VOLV Bcompany_announcement�ڎ�"WAB Volvo: Volvo and Waabi kick off customer operations in Texas, starting with Warp[TM]*XSTO2mfn:$4bdbd63e-8d48-568c-aba5-f7184a26a182Bxhttps://mfn.se/cis/a/volvo/ab-volvo-volvo-and-waabi-kick-off-customer-operations-in-texas-starting-with-warp-tm-4bdbd63eJenrvolvo��-Integration of the Volvo VNL Autonomous powered by the Waabi Driver into Warp's
network unlocks autonomy for LTL freight segment.

Volvo Autonomous Solutions (V.A.S.) and Waabi today announced the start of
autonomous operation for Warp in Texas, the first customer operation in the
Volvo-Waabi partnership. The Volvo VNL Autonomous powered by the Waabi Driver is
now transporting freight directly to third-party facilities along the
Dallas-Houston corridor. These operations will enable Warp, an open-source
freight network that provides less-than-truckload (LTL) and full-truckload (FTL)
freight services for carriers and shippers, to transport its customers' goods
autonomously along the corridor.

The start of customer operations between V.A.S. and Waabi marks a milestone in
the two companies' partnership. Integrating V.A.S.' autonomous transport
ecosystem Autona / freight into Warp's freight network creates opportunities to
optimize freight flows and support more efficient movement of goods as demand
grows. Autona / freight brings together the Volvo VNL Autonomous, Waabi's
autonomous driving technology, fleet and transport management, terminals and
customer endpoints, operations, and uptime support to deliver a complete
transport solution designed to scale. The Volvo VNL Autonomous is built from the
ground up for autonomy with redundancies for six safety-critical systems and
specifically designed to integrate autonomous driving systems from partners such
as Waabi. Currently autonomous hauls are run with an observer in the driver's
seat.

"Together with Warp and Waabi, we are bringing autonomous freight into customer
operations in one of logistics' most demanding segments. This shows how quickly
our ecosystem can adapt to new customers and more complex operations. What we
learn on the Dallas-Houston corridor will help us strengthen our partnership and
continuously improve the ecosystem as we scale," said Nils Jaeger, President of
Volvo Autonomous Solutions.

"Waabi and Volvo Autonomous Solutions are building toward a future where
autonomous trucks integrate seamlessly into the networks that move freight every
day," said Lior Ron, COO of Waabi. "Our work with Warp is the first milestone on
that path, demonstrating that the Volvo VNL Autonomous, powered by the Waabi
Driver, can take on the full range of real-world freight, from operating within
less-than-truckload networks to moving goods directly to third-party facilities,
meeting customers where they are. We're incredibly excited about what's ahead as
we continue building that future together with Volvo."

With customer operations underway, V.A.S. and Waabi will use real-world
experience from the Dallas-Houston lane to prepare for additional routes and
support the broader scaling of autonomous freight in the coming years.

Unlocking autonomy for one of freight's most operationally complex segments

LTL shipping requires dynamic networks to consolidate, haul, and distribute
goods from multiple customers on tightly coordinated schedules. By moving
freight directly between facilities, without handoffs, the Volvo VNL Autonomous
powered by the Waabi Driver gives LTL providers like Warp a more predictable way
to keep their networks on schedule.

"Working with Volvo Autonomous Solutions and Waabi demonstrates what Warp's
network was built for: bringing advanced technology into the everyday movement
of freight," said Daniel Sokolovsky, CEO of Warp. "The value of autonomy grows
when you can coordinate the freight around it. Warp brings together shipments
from multiple customers and aligns routes, capacity, and timing, so advances on
a single lane can benefit the broader network."




October 5, 2026




