VOLV Bcompany_announcement���";AB Volvo: Three million tonnes hauled autonomously by Volvo*XSTO2mfn:$a389be26-847f-5b1f-80dc-946418a2a8a2B^https://mfn.se/cis/a/volvo/ab-volvo-three-million-tonnes-hauled-autonomously-by-volvo-a389be26Jenrvolvo��In a new milestone achievement, Volvo Autonomous Solutions (V.A.S.) has hauled
more than 3 million tonnes of material autonomously in the mining and quarrying
segment. The milestone demonstrates the ability of autonomous transportation to
deliver consistent and reliable value to customers.

The material has been hauled across V.A.S.' current and completed customer sites
in Europe. Key customers include Brønnøy Kalk in Norway and Boliden Garpenberg
in Sweden. The first autonomous hauling for Brønnøy Kalk started in 2023, with a
milestone of 1 million tonnes hauled autonomously reached in 2025. In this
Norwegian quarry, V.A.S. handles the flow of material from a quarry to a crusher
with nine autonomous trucks on a five-kilometer route through long tunnels with
steep inclines. In Boliden's Garpenberg mine in Sweden, autonomous trucks were
hauling material from a quarry to a dynamic unloading area as part of a dam
reinforcement project.

"Reaching three million tonnes hauled autonomously is an important milestone
that demonstrates the maturity of our autonomous transport solution and the
value it delivers in customer operations. This achievement reflects years of
close collaboration between the Volvo Autonomous Solutions team and our
customers to develop, deploy and scale autonomous hauling in real-world
environments" says Ingo Stürmer, CTO at V.A.S.

A complete solution for autonomous hauling, factory-assembled

The transport provided to V.A.S.' mining and quarrying customers is Volvo's
transport-as-a-service solution for confined areas, Autona / earth. The solution
includes autonomy-enabled trucks integrated with the Volvo Virtual Driver,
infrastructure, operations, uptime services and fleet management, enabling
customers to implement autonomy without handling the technical complexities and
without major equipment investment. Benefits of Autona / earth include
optimizing transportation flows, enabling continuous operation, and improving
safety by removing people from potentially hazardous environments.

The autonomous trucks are manufactured at Volvo Group's Säffle facility,
enabling fast and consistent delivery that poises V.A.S. for faster customer
implementation and meeting the growing demand for autonomous hauling.



