VOLV Bcompany_announcement����"oAB Volvo: The science that brought green back to China's Loess Plateau awarded the 2026 Volvo Environment Prize*XSTO2mfn:$3bb1793f-9961-54e5-893a-e755cb9568dbB�https://mfn.se/cis/a/volvo/ab-volvo-the-science-that-brought-green-back-to-chinas-loess-plateau-awarded-the-2026-volvo-environment-prize-3bb1793fJenrvolvo��&Once stripped of vegetation and scarred by some of the world's most severe soil
erosion, China's Loess Plateau has undergone a remarkable ecological
transformation. Professor Bojie Fu, one of the scientists who helped make that
recovery possible, is the recipient of the 2026 Volvo Environment Prize.

The Loess Plateau was a major cradle of early Chinese civilization. But
centuries of intensive cultivation and overgrazing pushed this huge area towards
ecological collapse. Vegetation disappeared, soil was swept away, and vast
quantities of sediment poured into the Yellow River. The landscape became a
stark symbol of unsustainable land use.

Today, large areas of the plateau are green again.

Behind this dramatic revival lies one of the world's most ambitious
landscape-restoration efforts-and decades of scientific research into how
damaged ecosystems can be rebuilt without exhausting the scarce resources on
which both nature and people depend.

Professor Bojie Fu has played a decisive role in that work. Through extensive
field studies and landscape-scale analysis, he and his team have investigated
the complex relationships between land use, vegetation, water, soil,
biodiversity, and local livelihoods.

Fu is a Professor of Geography and Landscape Ecology at the Research Center for
Eco-Environmental Sciences at the Chinese Academy of Sciences. He and his
research team revealed both the potential and the risks of ecological
restoration. More vegetation can hold soil in place, capture carbon, and revive
biodiversity-but planting the wrong species, or planting too densely, can deepen
water scarcity and threaten the long-term survival of the restored landscape.

This evidence helped shift restoration strategies away from mass planting
campaigns and towards carefully adapted, sustainable land management. It
provided practical guidance on which species to plant, where they could thrive,
and how to balance restoration with agriculture and local communities' needs.

One major obstacle to restoration was resistance from farmers who feared for
their livelihoods if they could no longer keep livestock or cultivate the land
as before. The project, dubbed Grain for Green, helped overcome this resistance
by compensating local communities and farmers.

The Prize Jury recognizes Professor Fu's pioneering contributions to
social-ecological systems and sustainability science, as well as his exceptional
ability to connect local restoration with global sustainable development. The
Jury particularly highlights how his work on the Loess Plateau has created
science-based pathways for ecological recovery, human well-being, and effective
policy across the Global South.

The plateau's revitalization was neither simple nor swift. It was a complex,
long-term undertaking involving local communities, government agencies,
researchers, and international partners. Early demonstration projects received
World Bank funding, while most subsequent investment came from the Chinese
government.

Its success depended not only on sustained financial and political commitment,
but also on continuous scientific monitoring and a willingness to adapt when
evidence showed that established approaches were not working.

Professor Fu's research demonstrates how science can turn restoration ambitions
into lasting environmental change-and how a landscape once considered devastated
can be given a future.

The Volvo Environment Prize has been awarded annually since 1990 and is regarded
as one of the scientific world's most respected environmental prizes. Presented
by an independent foundation, the award consists of a hand-crafted diploma, a
glass sculpture, and SEK 1.5 million.

Professor Bojie Fu will receive the Volvo Environment Prize at a ceremony in
Stockholm, Nov 2, 2026.

More information about the laureate and the prize: www.environment-prize.com




